sionism frames The Potato Eaters, contrasting Van Gogh's focus on realism with the movement's emphasis on light and color."}, {"sourceSlug": "19th-century-europe", "sourceName": "19th Century Europe", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "the-potato-eaters", "targetName": "The Potato Eaters", "context": "The Potato Eaters occurs in 19th Century Europe, amidst significant social and economic changes due to industrialization."}], "places": [{"name": "Nuenen, Netherlands", "role": "Creation site"}, {"name": "Amsterdam, Netherlands", "role": "Major exhibition"}, {"name": "Paris, France", "role": "Cultural influence"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: The Potato Eaters", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "the-potato-eaters"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q154469", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Potato_Eaters"], "tags": ["Netherlands", "painting", "Europe", "Paintings & Visual Art", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "The Potato Eaters contributed to the historical record of the Modern period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to artistic heritage and cultural identity. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The Potato Eaters is significant for its realistic portrayal of peasant life, influencing the Social Realism movement and shaping public perception of art as a vehicle for social commentary.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-25T05:33:51Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#30145863654", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Painting by vincent van gogh Created by Vincent van Gogh. Created c. 1885. The Potato Eaters is a painting from Europe by Vincent van Gogh, created circa 1885. A moderately significant entity in the historical record. Relevant to cause-and-effect analysis.", "newValue": "The Potato Eaters, painted by Vincent van Gogh in 1885, stands as a seminal work in the canon of Western art, capturing the harsh realities of rural life in the Netherlands. This painting marks an important phase in Van Gogh’s evolution as an artist, reflecting his desire to represent the lives of peasants and the working class during a time when industrialization was rapidly changing Europe. Situated in the modern era, this piece is crucial for understanding the intersection of art and socio-economic conditions in 19th-century Europe, amidst the backdrop of the Industrial Revolution and the rise of Impressionism.

In The Potato Eaters, Van Gogh used a dark, earthy palette to depict a family gathered around a humble meal of potatoes, emphasizing the rawness of their existence. The work was created while he was living in Nuenen, a rural village where he immersed himself in the daily lives of peasants. The composition is notable for its focus on the figures’ expressions and the interplay of light and shadow, which enhances the emotional depth of the scene. This painting was not merely an aesthetic endeavor; it was a social commentary on the plight of the working class, highlighting the struggles and dignity of their labor.

